#document(size: 11pt, lang: "en")[
#title[UAT Test Document — Ksav]

#subtitle[User Acceptance Testing workbook]

This document is a UAT workbook for Ksav. Each section names a feature, shows what it can do in English, and gives a Hebrew exercise you can try. Compile it to PDF to confirm rendering.

#hrule

#hlevel(level: 1)[1. Text styling]

English text styling works inline: #bold[bold], #italic[italic], #uline[underline], #sthrough[strike], #scaps[small caps], #big[big], #tiny[tiny], #sup[superscript], #sub_[subscript], #mark[marked], and #color(rgb("#b91c1c"))[coloured text].

#hlevel(level: 2)[Hebrew exercise]

Try typing Hebrew with nikud: #bold[שָׁלוֹם] and #italic[עוֹלָם], then a mixed line: Hello #bold[שלום] world.

#hlevel(level: 1)[2. Headings and table of contents]

#hlevel(level: 2)[A second-level heading]

#hlevel(level: 3)[Third level]

#toc

#hlevel(level: 1)[3. Lists]

#bullets(
  item[First bullet],
  item[Second bullet with a nested list:
    #bullets(
      item[sub-item a],
      item[sub-item b],
    )],
  item[Third bullet],
)

#numbered(
  item[Step one],
  item[Step two],
  item[Step three],
)

Hebrew exercise: build a list of mishnayot using #bullets and #numbered with Hebrew items.

#hlevel(level: 1)[4. Tables]

#mktable(columns: 3,
  cell[Name], cell[Value], cell[Note],
  cell[Alpha], cell[1], cell[first],
  cell[Beta], cell[2], cell[second],
)

#hlevel(level: 1)[5. Notes and apparatus]

A sentence with a footnote#fnote[This is a footnote at the page foot.] and an endnote#endnote[This is an endnote collected at the end.].

#hlevel(level: 2)[Hebrew exercise]

Write a Hebrew sentence: בראשית ברא#fnote[הערה בעברית] אלקים, then add a sidenote in the margin.

#endnotes

#hlevel(level: 1)[6. Page setup and columns]

The document uses A4 by default. Try changing paper size and margins via #document.

#hlevel(level: 1)[7. Formulas]

Inline formula: $ x^2 + y^2 = z^2 $. Display:

$ integral_0^1 x^2 "dx" = 1/3 $

#hlevel(level: 1)[8. Hebrew text direction]

#hlevel(level: 2)[Exercise: mixed direction]

Type a paragraph mixing English and Hebrew: The word שלום means peace. Check that punctuation lands correctly.

#hlevel(level: 1)[9. Images]

(Image exercise: insert an image with #img and adjust width.)

#hlevel(level: 1)[10. Version history and review]

Mark text as #inserted[inserted] or #deleted[deleted]. Add a #comment_[text comment].

#hlevel(level: 1)[11. Side notes and margin]

Place a note in the margin: this sentence has a sidenote#sidenote[Margin note text.].

Wrap a passage for two-sided margin notes:

#twosided[Content with notes on both sides.]

#hlevel(level: 1)[12. Note channels]

Define channels and route notes:

#channel("peirush", placement: "foot", height: 3cm)

בראשית ברא#fnote(channel: "peirush")[עיין רש״י] אלקים.

#show_region("peirush")

#hlevel(level: 1)[13. Section endnotes]

#endnote_config

A section with endnotes collected per section.

#hlevel(level: 1)[14. Review and changes]

#review_config

Inserted #inserted[added text], deleted #deleted[removed text], and a #comment_[reviewer comment].

#hlevel(level: 1)[15. Hebrew exercises]

Exercise 1: Write a paragraph entirely in Hebrew with nikud.

Exercise 2: Mix RTL and LTR on one line: The value is 5 and שלום is peace.

Exercise 3: Create a numbered list of halachot in Hebrew.

Exercise 4: Add a footnote to a Hebrew word: תורה#fnote[תורה שבעל פה].

#hlevel(level: 1)[Conclusion]

All features above should compile and render. Report any mismatch.
]
EOF
/home/shaul/ksav/ksav/engine/target/release/ksav /home/shaul/ksav/UAT.ksav /tmp/uatout 2>&1 | head -40